  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes implantable bioelectronic interfaces for both the spinal cord and peripheral nerves, emphasizing where different device types are placed and what functions they provide. In the spinal cord, several layers are shown around the tissue, including pia, arachnoid, and dura mater, with blood vessels and spinal nerves nearby. Devices are positioned at different depths: epidural implants outside the dura, subdural arrays closer to the cord surface, and a multimodal fibre inserted into the tissue. These tools are depicted as enabling combinations of electrical recording, electrical stimulation, optical stimulation, biochemical stimulation, or optoelectronic stimulation. An “organic bioelectronics” approach appears focused mainly on biochemical delivery near the cord.\n\nOn the peripheral nerve side, the figure compares multiple interface designs wrapped around or inserted into branching nerves. A cuff implant encircles a nerve and supports recording plus electrical and optical stimulation. Regenerative and transversal arrays are shown as more invasive interfaces penetrating or bridging nerve tissue, mainly for electrical recording and stimulation. Overall, the image highlights a spectrum of neural implants, from surface-mounted to penetrating devices, and from single-mode electrical interfaces to more multifunctional systems integrating optical or chemical control.",
